You will be able to distinguish and know

00:02:43.185 --> 00:02:52.420
which logarithms are faster than others,

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.550
assuming that you have large inputs, because they are less convergent in terms of complexity.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:58.910
This is about efficient procedures.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:04.850
Okay, let's start talking about this chapter.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.800
When I say large inputs, I mean things

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.720
like the US highway system, a map

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.110
of all the highways in the United States of America. The

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:17.850
human genome, which has a billion letters

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:23.170
in the alphabet. Social network response on Facebook, I

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.840
think 500 million contracts or so.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.280
Therefore, this is a large input.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.470
Now we have a major definition that has really changed with the times. And so the

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.440
true definition of the 21st century

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:36.971
is, I think, a trillion dollars.

00:03:36.971 --> 00:03:37.470
right? Back

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.680
when you were your age, it was as big as 1,000.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.400
[Laughter]

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.844
I think my history dates back here. Back

00:03:44.844 --> 00:03:46.760
when your Eric was your age, he was a million.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:47.260
right?

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.650
[Laughter]

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:55.000
But what's really happening is the world is moving faster, and

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.420
things are getting bigger.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.880
We have the capacity and computing power for large inputs,

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.220
but that doesn't mean efficiency

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:05.760
isn't a critical concern. The

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.690
reality is that you can, perhaps,

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:13.550
scan a billion items in a few seconds.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:17.750
But if you have an algorithm that requires cubic complexity,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.899
suddenly you're not talking about 10 raised to 9,

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:22.079
you're talking about 10 to 27. And

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.510
even current computers can't really

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:30.890
handle those kinds of numbers, so efficiency is a concern.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:34.820
As the inputs get larger, it becomes more of a concern.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:35.320
Good?

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.398
Therefore, we are concerned about the

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:51.310
efficiency of procedures for solving problems on a large scale

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:51.940
in this class.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.640
We are concerned about the plausibility,

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:07.030
because - just as, you know, 1000

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.600
was a big number a few decades ago, and

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.140
now it's kind of a small number - it's quite

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:16.430
possible that by the time you guys professors

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:18.220
teaching this class at some universities are going to find that

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.690
trillion is going to be a small number.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.430
We will talk about-- I don't know -

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:27.520
10 raised to 18 as something that we are

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:32.620
concerned about from the point of view of a common

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.510
input issue for an algorithm.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:38.120
Therefore, gradual progression is important.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.480
We want to be able to track how our algorithms are going to

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.000
do as the inputs get bigger and bigger.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:52.180
You are going to learn a handful of different data structures.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.650
We will call them classic data structures,

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:01.450
such as binary search trees, hash tables - that's

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:06.020
called dictionaries in Python - and data

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.470
structures - such as balanced binary search trees - that

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:12.975
are more efficient than just ordinary binary search trees.

00:06:12.975 --> 00:06:14.350
These are all data structures

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:18.540
that were invented many decades ago.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.850
But they have stood the test of time, and

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:23.530
they are still useful.

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.210
We are on our way to increasing these data structures in different ways

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:30.330
